<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/advanced/media-law-and-ethics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Media law and ethics are the newsroom\'s <strong>operating constraints</strong> — the law
    lesson\'s frameworks applied to publishing, plus the professional codes that go beyond legal
    minimum (legal is not the same as ethical: both newsworthy and kind is the test).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="defamation, defences, privacy, sources and secrecy" widget="equationStep" config={lawCfg} />

  <h2>The working rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pre-publication review is a discipline:</strong> reading copy against the defamation elements before publishing; the correction (prominent, fast) is both an ethical duty and the best legal mitigation — corrections pages are trust infrastructure (the verification lesson\'s transparency, legalised).</li>
    <li><strong>The SPJ code\'s four pillars:</strong> seek truth and report it; minimise harm; act independently; be accountable — minimise harm is the pillar law ignores: naming victims, suicide-reporting guidelines (WHO standards reduce contagion), children in coverage (the ethics lesson\'s principles, newsroom-grade).</li>
    <li><strong>Copyright and fair use:</strong> quoting and criticism (fair dealing/fair use) vs reproducing — the IP lesson\'s boundaries; aggregation with attribution is etiquette, not a licence.</li>
    <li><strong>SLAPP suits are the modern threat:</strong> strategic lawsuits against public participation — deep-pocketed subjects burying outlets in litigation costs; anti-SLAPP statutes (and the EU directive, 2024) are the legal system\'s immune response. Journalism\'s legal environment is a press-freedom index, tracked yearly (RSF, CPJ).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NYT v Sullivan (1964); the Leveson Report (2012); SPJ Code of Ethics; RSF Press Freedom Index.</p>
</TopicPage>
